# Vendored third-party sources are checked out byte-for-byte on every platform
# (no CRLF conversion), so each vendor's anchored checksums (VENDOR.json,
# verified by the vendoring tests) match on Windows too. Without this a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF->CRLF and every checksum drifts.
third_party/** -text

# Every entry in CHANGELOG.md is inserted at the same anchor — the top of
# `## [Unreleased]` → `### Fixed` — so any two pull requests open at the same
# time edit the same region and git stops on a conflict. That is not an
# occasional collision: 17 of the 30 pull requests before this one touched the
# file, and each merge invalidates every other open branch, so N concurrent PRs
# cost N-1 rebases on a file that has never affected behavior. Four landed in
# one afternoon (#653, #656, #662, #663), each one blocking a PR whose code
# merged cleanly.
#
# `union` is git's built-in driver for exactly this shape: both sides' added
# lines are kept instead of conflicting. It does NOT understand the list, so it
# does not guarantee the newest entry lands on top — check the order when it
# resolves one, and expect to reorder occasionally. It also resolves silently,
# which is only safe because `check-merge-conflict` backs it up (issue #664 —
# that hook is currently a no-op in CI, so land its fix alongside this).
#
# This is a mitigation. The structural fix is one changelog fragment file per
# change, assembled at release, so two PRs never touch the same file at all —
# issue #668.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
